@startuml dyn-env-sleep
!include include/palette.puml

title Dynamic: runbook env-sleep decides, then stops the cluster of a tier (ADR-IR33, ADR-IR34, §7.0, §7.2)

<style>
activityDiagram {
  activity {
    BackgroundColor #FFFFFF
    LineColor $C_OCTOPUS
    MaximumWidth 380
  }
  diamond {
    BackgroundColor #FFFFFF
    LineColor $C_OCTOPUS
  }
  partition {
    LineColor $C_OCTOPUS
    FontColor $C_OCTOPUS
  }
  arrow {
    LineColor #555555
  }
  note {
    MaximumWidth 520
  }
  .azure {
    BackgroundColor $C_AZURE
    FontColor #FFFFFF
    LineColor $C_AZURE
  }
  .sleep {
    BackgroundColor $C_WAKE
    FontColor #FFFFFF
    LineColor $C_WAKE
  }
  .stay {
    BackgroundColor $C_GLOBAL_TIER
    FontColor #FFFFFF
    LineColor $C_GLOBAL_TIER
  }
}
</style>

start
:Runbook env-sleep of platform-infrastructure in infra-<tier>.
Started hourly at minute 0 by trigger env-sleep-hourly-<tier>
(prompts at their defaults), or by hand with the prompts
Sleep.Force, Sleep.DryRun and Sleep.NowOverride;
note right
  Concurrency tag
  <environment-id>/cluster-power,
  shared with env-wake: the two never
  run at once in one environment
end note
partition "Step Decide sleep\nAzure script on hosted-ubuntu;\ntasks read with Platform.OctopusApiKey" {
  :Output Sleep.DryRun: true only
  when the prompt says true;
  if (Sleep.NowOverride set?) then (yes)
    if (Sleep.DryRun?) then (true)
      :now = Sleep.NowOverride;
    else (false)
      :Ignored (warning);
    endif
  else (no)
  endif
  if (Sleep.Enabled
not true?) then (yes)
    :stay: disabled; <<stay>>
  elseif (Task queued or
executing?) then (yes)
    :stay: busy; <<stay>>
  elseif (Sleep.Force
true?) then (yes)
    :sleep: forced; <<sleep>>
  elseif (Sleep hold ahead
of now?) then (yes)
    :stay: held; <<stay>>
  elseif (Outside the
working window?) then (yes)
    :sleep: outside
the window; <<sleep>>
  elseif (Idle for
Sleep.IdleMinutes?) then (yes)
    :sleep: idle; <<sleep>>
  else (no)
    :stay: active; <<stay>>
  endif
  :Outputs Sleep.Decision
  (sleep or stay) and Sleep.Reason,
  logs the decision in one line;
  note right
    Rules in order; the first match decides.
    1. Sleep.Enabled is true in both tiers. When false, a forced run stays too.
    2. Busy: a deployment or runbook run of any project is Queued, Executing or Cancelling in tdd and uat (nonprod) or prod, or in infra-<tier>. This run is excluded, and a run queued over 15 minutes ahead is ignored. Sleep.Force never overrides this rule. An unreadable task list fails the run, and nothing stops.
    3. Sleep.Force: prompt, default false. It skips rules 4 to 6.
    4. Sleep hold: tags platform-sleep-hold-until (ISO 8601) and platform-sleep-hold-by on rg-platform-<tier>-aks, read with az group show as azure-platform-lifecycle-<tier>; set and released by runbook sleep-hold (conformance-arm, teardown). A value that is not ISO 8601 or lies more than 12 hours ahead is ignored with a warning; an unreadable group fails the run, and nothing stops.
    5. Working window: Sleep.WorkDays Mon to Fri, Sleep.WorkdayStart 07:00 to Sleep.WorkdayEnd 19:00, Sleep.TimeZone America/Chicago. Skipped with a warning when the step container lacks the time zone.
    6. Idle: the newest completed task in those environments, env-sleep runs excluded, is older than Sleep.IdleMinutes (120), or none exists.
  end note
}
partition "Step Stop cluster" {
  if (Run condition (variable):
no error and Sleep.Decision = sleep?) then (no)
    :Step skipped; the cluster
    keeps running;
    stop
  else (yes)
  endif
  if (Sleep.DryRun?) then (true)
    :Logs "would stop now",
    nothing changes;
    stop
  else (false)
  endif
  :az aks show aks-platform-<tier>; <<azure>>
  note right
    Azure script on hosted-ubuntu as
    azure-platform-lifecycle-<tier>
    (OIDC, id-platform-lifecycle-<tier>);
    task list read with Platform.OctopusApiKey
  end note
  if (Power state?) then (other)
    :Stopped or missing: nothing to stop.
    Starting, Stopping, Updating:
    the next hourly run decides;
    stop
  else (Running)
  endif
  :Enable apr-sleep-<tier> (warning if missing).
  A trap disables it again on any exit
  before the stop is accepted; <<azure>>
  if (Task list read again:
task queued or executing?) then (yes)
    :Disable apr-sleep-<tier> again; exit; <<azure>>
    stop
  else (no)
  endif
  :az aks stop --no-wait; <<azure>>
  if (Stop accepted?) then (no)
    :Disable apr-sleep-<tier> again,
    fail the run; <<azure>>
    end
  else (yes)
  endif
  :Stopping. apr-sleep-<tier> stays
  enabled while the cluster sleeps; <<sleep>>
}
stop

legend right
  |<$C_WAKE>    | Sleep decision or stop |
  |<$C_GLOBAL_TIER>    | Stay awake |
  |<$C_AZURE>    | Azure CLI call (lifecycle identity) |
endlegend
@enduml
